I've just downloaded the binary version of nethack for Linux.
(This is the first time I've tried to use a binary, instead of
building from sources, but I've been thinking about
experimenting with hearse, and want a standard binary for that.)

The first question is how one uses color effectively.  In the
past, I've always hacked my sources before building, to ensure
that all of the colors I got were visible---the default version
seems to display my dog in white, which isn't visible on a white
background, and to display some other things in very dark colors,
which aren't visible on dark backgrounds.  What combination of
terminal background/foreground colors should I use for best
results?  (Or is there some trick I'm not aware of to configure
the colors without modifying the sources and rebuilding---I've
got a 64 bit system, and by default, if I rebuild, I'll have a
64 bit version of nethack, and the bones won't be compatible
with hearse.)

Also, for some strange reason, in a standard xterm, with $TERM
set to xterm, nethack seems to not send the leading escape when
an escape sequence follows another immediately.  It does this
correctly in the other types of terminal windows, e.g. aterm
(with $TERM set to rxvt) and in an xterm window if $TERM is set
to rxvt (which seems to work as well), so I suppose it is a
problem with my termcaps, but I'll admit that it's been some 20
years since I last fiddled with termcaps, and I'm no longer
really sure about where to begin looking.  (It's not a big
problem---I just set TERM to rxvt, and get on with it---, but
I am rather curious.)

